== Confirmation

I #smallcaps[want] you to notice the #smallcaps[security which Paul confidently expected] for all the saints. He says, “Who will confirm you#super[+] to the end, so that you#super[+] will be blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.” This is the kind of confirmation which is to be desired above all things. You see, it assumes that the people are in the right, and it sets out to confirm them in the right. It would be an awful thing to confirm a man in ways of sin and error. Think of a confirmed drunkard, or a confirmed thief, or a confirmed liar. It would be a deplorable thing for a man to be confirmed in unbelief and ungodliness. Divine confirmation can be enjoyed only by those to whom the grace of God has already been shown. It is the work of the Holy Spirit. He who gives faith strengthens and establishes it: he who kindles love in us preserves it and increases its flame. What he makes us know by his first teaching, the good Spirit causes us to know with greater clearness and certainty by still further instruction. Holy acts are confirmed till they become habits, and holy feelings are confirmed till they become settled states. Experience and practice confirm our beliefs and our resolutions. Both our joys and our sorrows, our successes and our failures, are sanctified to the very same end; just as the tree is helped to root itself both by the soft showers and by the rough winds. The mind is instructed, and in its growing knowledge it gathers reasons for persevering in the good way: the heart is comforted, and so it is made to cling more closely to the consoling truth. The grip grows tighter, and the step grows firmer, and the man himself becomes more solid and substantial.

This is not a merely natural growth, but is as distinct a work of the Spirit as conversion. #emph[The Lord will surely give it to those who are relying on him for eternal life]. By his inward working he will deliver us from being “unstable as water,” and cause us to be rooted and grounded. It is part of the way he saves us — this building us up into Christ Jesus and causing us to abide in him. Dear reader, you may look for this every day; and you will not be disappointed. He whom you trust will make you like a tree planted by streams of water, kept so well that even your leaf will not wither.

What a strength a confirmed Christian is to a church! He is a comfort to the sorrowful, and a help to the weak. Would you not like to be one? Confirmed believers are pillars in the house of our God. They are not carried away by every wind of doctrine, nor overthrown by sudden temptation. They are a great stay to others, and act as anchors in times of trouble in the church. You who are beginning the holy life hardly dare to hope that you will become like them. But you need not fear; the good Lord will work in you as well as in them. One of these days you who are now a babe in Christ will be a father in the church. Hope for this great thing; but hope for it as a gift of grace, and not as the wages of work, or as the product of your own energy.

The inspired apostle Paul speaks of these people as to be confirmed #emph[to the end]. He expected the grace of God to preserve them personally to the end of their lives, or till the Lord Jesus came. Indeed, he expected that the whole church of God in every place and in all time would be kept to the end of this age, till the Lord Jesus as the Bridegroom comes to celebrate the wedding feast with his perfected Bride. All who are in Christ will be confirmed in him till that glorious day. Has he not said, “Because I live, you#super[+] also will live”? He also said, “I give My sheep eternal life, and they will never perish. No one can snatch them out of My hand.” He who has begun a good work in you#super[+] will confirm it until the day of Christ. The work of grace in the soul is not a surface reformation; the life planted in the new birth comes from a living and incorruptible seed, which lives and abides forever; and God’s promises to believers are not of a passing kind, but their fulfillment includes the believer’s holding to his way till he comes to endless glory. We are kept by the power of God, through faith, to salvation. “The righteous will hold to his way.” Not as the result of our own merit or strength, but as a gift of free and undeserved favor, those who believe are “kept in Jesus Christ.” Of the sheep of his fold Jesus will lose none; no member of his body will die; no gem of his treasure will be missing on the day when he gathers up his jewels. Dear reader, the salvation which is received by faith is not a thing of months and years; for our Lord Jesus has “obtained #emph[eternal] salvation for us,” and what is eternal cannot come to an end.

Paul also declares his expectation that the Corinthian saints would be “confirmed to the end, #emph[blameless].” This blamelessness is a precious part of our keeping. To be kept holy is better than merely to be kept safe. It is a dreadful thing when you see religious people blundering from one disgrace into another; they have not believed in the power of our Lord to make them blameless. The lives of some professing Christians are a series of stumbles; they are never quite down, and yet they are seldom steady on their feet. This is not fitting for a believer; he is invited to walk with God, and #emph[by faith he can attain steady perseverance in holiness; and he ought to do so]. The Lord is able not only to save us from hell, but to keep us from falling. We need not yield to temptation. Is it not written, “Sin shall not have dominion over you#super[+]”? The Lord is able to keep the feet of his saints; and he will do it if we will trust him to do so. We need not defile our garments; by his grace we may keep them unstained by the world; we are bound to do this, “for without holiness no one will see the Lord.”

The apostle prophesied for these believers what he would have us seek — that we may be preserved “blameless in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.” Our Revised Version has “unreproveable,” instead of “blameless.” Possibly a better rendering would be “unimpeachable.” God grant that on that last great day we may stand free from every charge, so that no one in the whole universe may dare to challenge our claim to be the redeemed of the Lord. We have sins and weaknesses to mourn over, but these are not the kind of faults which would prove us to be outside of Christ; we will be clear of hypocrisy, deceit, hatred, and delight in sin; for these things would be fatal charges. Despite our failings, the Holy Spirit can work in us a character spotless before men; so that, like Daniel, we will give accusing tongues no grounds, except in the matter of our religion. Multitudes of godly men and women have shown lives so transparent, so consistent throughout, that no one could gainsay them. The Lord will be able to say of many a believer, as he did of Job when Satan stood before him, “Have you considered My servant, a man who is blameless and upright, who fears God and shuns evil?” This is what my reader must aim at, and look for at the Lord’s hands. This is the triumph of the saints — to keep following the Lamb wherever he goes, maintaining our integrity as before the living God. May we never turn aside into crooked ways, and give the adversary cause to blaspheme. Of the true believer it is written, “He keeps himself, and the evil one does not touch him.” May it be written so of us!

Friend, you who are just beginning in the divine life, the Lord can give you an irreproachable character. Even though in your past life you may have gone far into sin, the Lord can completely deliver you from the power of former habit, and make you an example of virtue. He can not only make you moral, but he can make you abhor every false way and follow after all that is saintly. Do not doubt it. The chief of sinners need not fall one bit behind the purest of the saints. Believe for this, and according to your faith it will be done to you.

Oh, what a joy it will be to be found blameless in the day of judgment! We are not wrong to join in singing that charming hymn:

#quote[“Bold shall I stand in that great day, \ For who aught to my charge shall lay; \ While through thy blood absolved I am, \ From sin’s tremendous curse and shame?”]

What bliss it will be to enjoy that dauntless courage, when heaven and earth flee away from the face of the Judge of all! This bliss will be the portion of everyone who looks only to the grace of God in Christ Jesus, and in that sacred strength wages continual war with all sin.
